---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('grade-11-scholarship-timeline');

const faqJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  'mainEntity': [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'What is my Grade 11 average worth in scholarships?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'The Alexander Rutherford Scholarship pays $500 for a Grade 11 five-course average of 75 to 79.9%, and $800 for 80% or higher. There is no competition for it. Grade 11 marks also drive early admission and entrance scholarship offers, which are calculated before your Grade 12 finals exist.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Can Grade 11 students apply for scholarships in Alberta?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Yes, several. The Horatio Alger Association of Canada Scholarship is open only to Grade 11 students, the University of Lethbridge Grade 11 Merit Award pays on a 75% average, and South Country Co-op takes applicants as early as Grade 10.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Is it better to join many clubs or focus on one activity?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Focus on one. Applications reward sustained involvement, not variety: two years of the same volunteer role with growing responsibility beats eight one-off activities. Grade 11 is the last year you can start something and still call it sustained by application season.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'What should I do in Grade 11 to prepare for scholarships?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Protect your five-course average, go deep on one activity rather than joining many, participate in the classes of teachers you might later ask for a reference, build your award list and turn on deadline reminders, and draft one essay in June while the year is still fresh.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Do I need a 90% average to win scholarships?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'No. Many local awards weigh involvement and need as well as marks, and each draws from one town or school.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJson)} slot="ld" />

  <p>
    Most scholarship applications are due in Grade 12, but the marks, activities and references they ask about are mostly set by the end of Grade 11. This is what counts a year out, and what does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Grade 11 marks are worth money</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Alexander Rutherford Scholarship</a> pays $500 for a Grade 11 five-course average of 75 to 79.9%, and $800 for 80% or higher. Reach the average and it pays. Grade 11 marks also drive early admission and entrance scholarship offers, which are calculated before your Grade 12 finals exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Pick one thing and go deep</h2>
  <p>
    Applications reward sustained involvement, not variety. Two years of the same volunteer role, with growing responsibility, beats eight one-off activities. Grade 11 is the last year you can start something and still call it "sustained" by application season. It does not need to be impressive.
  </p>

  <h2>Bank your reference relationships</h2>
  <p>
    The teachers who write your reference letters in Grade 12 are the ones who know you from Grade 11. Participate in the classes of teachers you might ask, and stay in touch with coaches and supervisors. When the time comes, our <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference letter guide</a> covers the ask itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Build the list before you need it</h2>
  <p>
    Fifteen minutes this year:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Run <a href="/match/">Build my combo</a> to see what you already qualify for. Some awards accept Grade 11 applicants outright: the <a href="/scholarships/horatio-alger-canadian-scholarships/">Horatio Alger Canadian Scholarships</a> are <em>only</em> open to Grade 11s, the <a href="/scholarships/university-of-lethbridge-grade-11-merit-award/">University of Lethbridge Grade 11 Merit Award</a> pays on a 75% average, and <a href="/scholarships/south-country-co-op-scholarship/">South Country Co-op</a> takes applicants as early as Grade 10.</li>
    <li>Save the Grade 12 awards you like and <a href="/saved/">turn on deadline reminders</a>.</li>
    <li>Note which big awards need things you can still build: a portfolio, hours, a specific course. The <a href="/scholarships/atco-indigenous-education-high-school-merit-award/">ATCO Indigenous Education High School Merit Award</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/red-deer-polytechnic-building-on-excellence-academic-award/">Red Deer Polytechnic entrance scholarship</a> both reward averages you are setting right now.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start one essay in June</h2>
  <p>
    After finals, while the year is fresh, write one draft answering "tell us about a challenge you faced" using something true from this year. In the fall, <a href="/guides/how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay/">that draft</a> becomes every application's starting point, so in October you are editing instead of starting.
  </p>

  <h2>What not to do</h2>
  <p>
    Do not join five clubs in September of Grade 12 to fill a form. Do not pay for scholarship search services; every award worth having is findable free. And do not write off scholarships because your average is not in the 90s. Many local awards weigh involvement and need as well as marks, and <a href="/guides/local-scholarships-better-odds/">local awards are open to far fewer students</a>.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
